        "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Linear Rational Function",
        "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{5x+2}\\textrm{d}x}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let $u=5x+2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{5x+2}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{5x+2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|5x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948072/example-449.png"
      }
